Correction: the transcription above is incomplete and I have set out a corrected version below.

<page type="text">
  <layout>One page of text in a single column. Margin note at upper left ("nepo-te."). Below the text, a heading in italics ("Interpretatio Grammatica."), then a paragraph with a section mark in the left margin, running on to the next page.</layout>
  <page_number>106</page_number>
  <running_head>[illegible, ~8 letters] I</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <p>» EO PRONEPOS, &amp;c. Sed etsi uiuo filio, iā mortuo {*}pro nepote, cuius uxor prægnas effet, testamētum faceret, po[?] test dicere, SI ME VIVO FILIVS DECEDAT, TVNC QVI PRONEPOS. Num si &amp; filius &amp; nepos uiuat, concipere utrisque mortuis, uiuo se, tunc qui prone-pos nasceretur? quod simili-ter admittendum est. ita sa-nè, si prius nepos, deinde fi-lius decederet, ne successio-ne testamentū rumperetur.<note letter="*" side="left">nepo-te.</note></p>
    <head level="2">Interpretatio Grammatica.</head>
    <p><note side="left">§[?]</note>Idem credendum est.) Tres proponit casus, quibus instituit pronepos posset, ad quos hanc pertinere formulam demonstrat: cum aut filio mortuo nepos, aut</p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Line-initial » marks in the printed quotation are shown once at the start of the paragraph; they stand at the beginning of every line in the scan. "po[?] test" is letter-spaced in the scan and uncertain. The running head and page number are partly illegible ("106" may be "206"). The margin mark beside "Idem credendum est." is a section sign in a ring, uncertain. The text is cut off at the foot of the page and continues on the next page. Some hyphens are printed at line ends, others are not. The word "mortuis" is printed split across lines without a hyphen.</notes>
</page>